Q1. What is Polymorphism? Why do we need it?

Interview Ready Answer

Polymorphism means

"One Name, Many Forms."

It allows the same method, interface or reference
to exhibit different behavior depending on the object
or parameters.

Why do we need it?
• Code Reusability.
• Flexibility.
• Extensibility.
• Reduce Code Duplication.
• Runtime Decision Making.
• Loose Coupling.

Types of Polymorphism

1. Compile-Time (Static) Polymorphism

Achieved using

• Method Overloading
• Constructor Overloading

Compiler decides which method/constructor to call.

------------------------------------------------------------

2. Runtime (Dynamic) Polymorphism

Achieved using

• Inheritance
• Method Overriding
• Upcasting

JVM decides which overridden method executes.

Real-Life Example

One person can have multiple roles.

• Teacher in school
• Father at home
• Customer in a shop

Software Industry Example

interface Payment{

    void pay();
}

class UpiPayment implements Payment{

    public void pay(){

        System.out.println("UPI Payment");
    }
}

class CardPayment implements Payment{

    public void pay(){

        System.out.println("Card Payment");
    }
}

Payment p=new UpiPayment();

p.pay();

